# Platform & SDKs Overview

## What is this section?

This section explains **how to implement a mobile feature on Android, iOS, or React Native**. It translates the Architecture section's boundaries into platform code: screens, ViewModels/state owners, use cases, repositories, and data adapters.

It also covers shared application foundations and the SDK catalog. An **SDK** is a software development kit supplied by a provider or platform team. Its package, supported versions, configuration, and API must be confirmed before integration.

**React Native is preferred for new applications.** Android and iOS remain supported. When joining an existing app, follow its assigned stack. React Native uses **Redux Toolkit where Redux state is required**.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep enterprise code, SDK contracts, configuration, and evidence within approved systems. Never include credentials, signing material, personal data, or sensitive logs in documentation, screenshots, or AI prompts.

## When do you use it?

| Your task | Open |
|---|---|
| Build or change a Kotlin/Compose feature | [Android Standards](./native-android-standards.mdx) |
| Build or change a Swift/SwiftUI feature | [iOS Standards](./native-ios-standards.mdx) |
| Build or change a React Native feature with Redux Toolkit | [React Native Standards](./react-native-standards.mdx) |
| Apply shared application behavior | [App Foundations](./enterprise-app-foundations.mdx) |
| Find a documented internal SDK and its integration contract | [Master SDK Catalog](./master-sdk-catalog.mdx) |

The catalog currently includes **Authentication SDK only**; its team-provided technical contract remains pending. Do not infer a package name, installation command, or SDK method from a foundation example.

Machine installation belongs in Onboarding. Build automation, real-device execution, Datadog setup, and store delivery remain in their dedicated sections.

## Understand MVVM before copying the example

**MVVM means Model–View–ViewModel.** The View displays state and emits user actions. The ViewModel owns presentation state and coordinates the feature. The Model side contains the domain rules and data-access abstractions used by the feature.

Clean Architecture adds explicit dependency boundaries: business rules depend on contracts, while concrete repositories and adapters implement those contracts outside the domain.

| Responsibility | Plain meaning | Chicken-order example |
|---|---|---|
| **View** | Displays state and collects input. | Quantity field, Place Order button, progress, and confirmation. |
| **ViewModel / state owner** | Coordinates actions and exposes UI-facing state. | Blocks overlapping taps and tracks submitting, confirmed, invalid, or unknown status. |
| **Use case** | Applies the feature's business rules through domain contracts. | Validates the selected quantity before submission. |
| **Repository** | Coordinates data access and maps results. | Calls the approved order adapter and returns a domain outcome. |
| **API adapter** | Performs the actual request/response integration. | Uses the approved authenticated backend contract. |
| **Composition** | Connects concrete dependencies. | Injects the API into the repository, then the use case into the state owner. |

The backend authorizes and processes real orders. It is a separate service, not part of the mobile app's ViewModel or data layer.

## Follow the example: place a chicken order

We use **My Chicken App** across the platform guides so you can compare the same feature on all three platforms.

1. The user enters a quantity and taps **Place Order**.
2. The state owner exposes **submitting** and blocks overlapping taps.
3. The use case validates the request. Invalid input returns feedback without an API call.
4. The repository uses the injected API adapter and maps the response.
5. The state owner publishes the result to the View.

