| Result | What the user sees |
|---|---|
| **Invalid input** | Validation feedback; no order was submitted by that validation path. |
| **Confirmed order** | The confirmed result belonging to the current account and request. |
| **Unknown outcome** | A verification/recovery state. Do not automatically submit another order. |

A timeout can occur after the backend accepted an order. Disabling a button, cancelling a task, or generating a request ID alone does not provide durable duplicate protection. Real submissions require the approved backend idempotency and reconciliation contract.

The copyable demo uses quantity **1–20**, item `chicken-grilled`, and receipt `order-001`. These are synthetic teaching values. **The demo API makes no real order or payment.** Actual product limits, pricing, inventory, endpoints, authorization, and response schemas require confirmed contracts.

## See how MVVM maps to each platform

| Part | Android | iOS | React Native |
|---|---|---|---|
| **View** | Compose screen | SwiftUI view | React Native components |
| **ViewModel responsibility** | ViewModel + read-only StateFlow | Main-actor observable ViewModel | Redux Toolkit slice/thunk with typed subscriptions |
| **Business operation** | Kotlin use case | Swift use case | TypeScript use case |
| **Data boundary** | Kotlin interfaces and adapters | Swift protocols and adapters | TypeScript contracts and adapters |
| **Dependency wiring** | Existing DI/Hilt composition | Application composition | Existing application/store dependency configuration |

For React Native, Redux Toolkit fulfils the presentation-state role in the example. Do not add a second mutable ViewModel that duplicates the store. Keep temporary input state local where the application's ownership requirements allow it.

## Use the reference files

Start with the platform guide for your assigned app. The [complete chicken-order MVVM reference](./chicken-order-mvvm-reference.mdx) supplies all demo files in Android, iOS, and React Native tabs, including folder locations, entry points, expected behavior, and production integration boundaries.

The reference is a working teaching implementation with an injected fake. It is not a confirmed enterprise order SDK or an application-wide production certification. Its verification scope and remaining native checks are stated in that page.

