# Android Standards — MVVM Chicken Order

## 1. What is this guide?

Use this guide to build or change a **native Android feature in Kotlin**. It explains the platform responsibilities, then supplies a complete chicken-order demo you can mount in an existing Compose application.

**React Native remains preferred for new applications.** Follow the assigned platform for an existing app. This page implements the Android-specific MVVM pattern; it does not require a framework migration.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use synthetic accounts and isolated test environments. Keep enterprise APIs, credentials, signing material, and sensitive diagnostics out of code examples, screenshots, and AI prompts.

## 2. What do we use, and why?

| Technology / pattern | What it does | Where you use it |
|---|---|---|
| **Kotlin** | Expresses typed models, operations, and failures. | Domain, repository, data, and presentation code. |
| **Jetpack Compose** | Renders UI from observable state. | Quantity input, progress, buttons, and order result. |
| **ViewModel + StateFlow** | Retains and exposes screen state under the appropriate lifecycle owner. | Coordinates submission and blocks overlapping taps. |
| **Coroutines** | Runs asynchronous work with explicit ownership and cancellation. | ViewModel scope and suspend repository/API calls. |
| **Use case** | Applies business validation independently of the UI. | Quantity and request validation before submission. |
| **Repository + API adapter** | Separates data policy/mapping from actual transport. | Converts the approved response into a domain outcome. |
| **Hilt / existing composition** | Supplies dependencies at their intended scopes. | Production application wiring. The isolated demo below uses a manual factory. |
| **Room, when required** | Persists structured local data. | Only features with an approved persistence policy; not needed for this demo. |

Use the existing approved versions. Android Gradle Plugin, Gradle wrapper, Kotlin/Compose compiler, JDK, SDK, and libraries must form a supported combination. The [Android architecture guidance](https://developer.android.com/topic/architecture) explains platform separation; the use-case boundary here is an enterprise convention.

## 3. Understand the chicken-order example

The user enters quantity **2**, taps **Place demo order**, sees progress, then a synthetic confirmation **order-001**. Invalid input produces feedback without an API call. An unknown outcome blocks another submission.

**Teaching assumptions:** quantity 1–20, item `chicken-grilled`, account `demo-account`. The fake API performs no network request, real order, payment, or inventory update. Product rules and private service contracts must be confirmed before production integration.

