## 8. Replace the demo integration deliberately

| Boundary | Production action |
|---|---|
| **API** | Implement `OrderApi` using the approved authenticated client, endpoint, response schema, timeout, and circuit-breaker contract. No private endpoint or SDK method is invented here. |
| **Result mapping** | Validate the real order/account/request association and known rejection semantics. A nonblank ID is only the demo's mapping rule. |
| **Retry safety** | Confirm server idempotency and reconciliation before replaying an order. Cancellation and timeouts do not prove the backend rejected it. |
| **Session ownership** | Call `invalidateOwner()` when ending the account's feature ownership, detach the old UI, and coordinate unresolved writes before constructing a new owner. The method blocks stale UI publication; it is not a complete logout/recovery implementation. |
| **Process termination** | Persist/recover unresolved operations only through the approved design. In-memory state and ViewModel retention do not survive all process deaths or new instances. |
| **New order action** | Replace the demo reset with the actual business lifecycle. Never clear unknown state to bypass reconciliation. |
| **Shared foundations** | Use approved themes, localized resources, accessibility behavior, secure storage, feature flags, and observability integration. English strings and direct demo styling are teaching UI. |
| **Production wiring** | Remove demo account/API bindings. Use existing Hilt scopes and providers without duplicating session/network infrastructure. |

Keep network and persistence work safe to invoke from the main thread. A suspend declaration alone does not move blocking work off that thread. Do not add a local order queue or Room database as an assumed offline fallback.

