# iOS Standards — MVVM Chicken Order

## 1. What is this guide?

Use this guide to build or change a **native iOS feature in Swift**. It explains the platform responsibilities, then provides a complete chicken-order demo to mount inside an existing SwiftUI application.

**React Native remains preferred for new applications.** Follow the assigned platform for an existing app. This example uses MVVM with explicit domain and data boundaries; it does not require you to migrate an established UIKit application.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use synthetic accounts and approved test environments. Keep enterprise API contracts, credentials, signing material, personal data, and sensitive diagnostics out of examples, screenshots, and AI prompts.

## 2. What do we use, and why?

| Technology / pattern | Purpose | Chicken-order responsibility |
|---|---|---|
| **Swift** | Typed models, validation, and operation results. | Request and outcome types, quantity validation, repository contract. |
| **SwiftUI** | Renders UI from observable state. | Quantity field, progress, buttons, and result message. |
| **Observation + ViewModel** | Exposes presentation state while keeping mutations controlled. | Main-actor submission coordination and stale-owner protection. |
| **Swift concurrency** | Expresses asynchronous work and cancellation with explicit ownership. | ViewModel-owned task and async use-case/repository/API calls. |
| **Use case** | Applies business rules independently of the view. | Validates input before calling the repository. |
| **Repository + API adapter** | Separates data coordination/mapping from concrete transport. | Maps the API response to an order outcome. |
| **Application composition** | Constructs and injects concrete dependencies. | Connects the demo API, repository, use case, and ViewModel. |
| **SwiftData / another approved store, when needed** | Persists permitted application data. | Not required for this example; never add an order queue as an assumed fallback. |

This reference targets **iOS 17+**, SwiftUI Observation, and **Swift 6 with complete concurrency checking**. Domain/data code uses nonisolated default actor isolation; UI-facing types explicitly use `@MainActor`. If your target has main-actor default isolation, follow the approved target/module configuration rather than disabling concurrency checks or adding unchecked Sendable conformances.

An `async` declaration does not automatically move blocking work away from the main actor. Real transport and parsing must follow the application's execution and isolation policy. See [Apple's model-data guidance](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/managing-model-data-in-your-app).

## 3. Understand the example

