# React Native Standards — MVVM Chicken Order

## 1. What is this guide?

Use this guide to build a **React Native feature in TypeScript with Redux Toolkit**. It explains the stack, then supplies the chicken-order screen, presentation state, business operation, repository, API port, and demo composition.

**React Native is preferred for new applications.** Shared application code still produces separate Android and iOS apps; native setup, permissions, signing, and verification remain platform-specific.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use synthetic accounts and approved test environments. Keep private endpoints, credentials, sensitive Redux state, personal data, and diagnostics out of examples, screenshots, logs, and AI prompts.

## 2. What do we use, and why?

| Technology / pattern | Purpose | Chicken-order responsibility |
|---|---|---|
| **TypeScript** | Checks contracts before runtime. | Typed requests, results, state, and dependencies. External responses still require runtime validation. |
| **React Native** | Renders native application UI through React components. | Quantity input, buttons, progress, and result message. |
| **Redux Toolkit** | Coordinates serializable state and asynchronous actions. | Submission state, duplicate-tap guard, and stale-owner checks. |
| **React Redux** | Connects components to Redux with subscriptions and dispatch. | Typed state selection and action dispatch in the screen. |
| **Use case** | Applies business rules independently of rendering/store details. | Validates the order request before repository access. |
| **Repository + API adapter** | Separates result mapping from actual transport. | Maps a validated response or uncertain outcome into domain values. |
| **Composition** | Supplies concrete dependencies at the application boundary. | Injects the API into the repository and operation into thunk dependencies. |
| **Approved storage adapter, when required** | Persists permitted data under an explicit policy. | Not needed for this demo; do not introduce an offline order queue by assumption. |

### What does MVVM mean here?

**View:** the React Native screen. **ViewModel responsibility:** the Redux Toolkit slice/thunk and typed subscriptions. **Model:** domain values, business operations, and data-access contracts.

Do not introduce a second mutable class that mirrors the same Redux state. Keep reducers synchronous and free of network/storage effects; thunks invoke injected operations. Keep temporary UI state local when it does not require a shared owner.

Use approved project versions and existing locks. This reference uses Redux Toolkit's `configureStore`, `createSlice`, and `createAsyncThunk`. See the [official Redux Toolkit guide](https://redux.js.org/toolkit/usage/usage-guide). Do not replace an established RTK Query read-cache pattern merely to copy this command-submission example.

## 3. Understand the chicken-order example

The user enters quantity **2**, taps **Place demo order**, sees progress, then receives synthetic confirmation **order-001**. Invalid input produces feedback without an API call. Unknown outcomes block another submission.

**Teaching assumptions:** quantity 1–20, item `chicken-grilled`, account `demo-account`. The fake API does not perform HTTP, payment, inventory mutation, or real order creation. Actual product rules and backend schemas require confirmation.

