# App Foundations — Shared Capabilities

## What is this guide?

App Foundations are the **shared capabilities used by product features**: theme, credential storage, language, notification routing, branding, and account cleanup. Use them instead of building a separate implementation inside every screen.

They support MVVM rather than replacing it. The View consumes theme and localized content; the ViewModel/use case uses injected services; application composition owns long-lived adapters and session cleanup.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep enterprise code, configuration, and evidence within approved systems. Use synthetic data; never share credentials, signing material, personal data, or sensitive diagnostics in examples, screenshots, logs, or AI prompts.

## Understand the example first

In **My Chicken App**, the user selects a quantity and submits an order using the platform's MVVM feature. Foundations supply the surrounding application behavior:

| Foundation | Use in the chicken-order feature | Important boundary |
|---|---|---|
| **Design & Theming** | Quantity field, button, progress, and result use semantic light/dark tokens. | Product components do not invent raw colors or a second theme owner. |
| **Security & Credentials** | The approved session/client integration obtains credentials through native-backed storage. | Credentials never enter order UI state, Redux, logs, or the order request example. |
| **Localization** | Translate quantity validation, submission messages, and quantity-dependent copy. | Use actual locale/plural resources; do not concatenate translated fragments. |
| **Push & Routing** | An approved notification can direct the current user to a permitted destination. | Validate the payload and authorize the destination. Delivery is not proof of order status. |
| **Branding** | Apply the approved application identity and asset/token set. | Only owner-approved brand choices; not arbitrary remote icon or color input. |
| **Account Cleanup** | On sign-out, gate protected UI, coordinate writers, and clear registered account-scoped state. | Cleanup does not cancel or erase a backend order. Preserve unresolved business-operation recovery under the approved policy. |

These are integration examples, not a confirmed enterprise order API or notification schema. The feature's actual rules and SDK contracts must come from its owner.

## How to use this page

1. Select a foundation tab below.
2. Read what it owns and the platform-specific code/resources it needs.
3. Bind the adapter in application composition and inject the contract where required.
4. Verify failure, lifecycle, and account behavior using the actual application.

The code includes reference adapters and explicit host contracts; not every block is a standalone application. Approved dependencies, generated assets, native configuration, and service bindings remain required. The SDK catalog is separate and currently contains Authentication SDK only, with technical details pending. Do not infer an internal SDK package from a foundation pattern.

