### Platform integration responsibilities

| Android | iOS | React Native |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Configure channels and notification runtime permission where required. | Configure capabilities, APNs registration, notification authorization, and delegates. | Complete both native setups and use the installed SDK's supported API style. |
| Separate notification/data payload behavior and foreground/background handling. | Treat foreground presentation, notification taps, cold launch, and background updates separately. | Handle foreground events, opened-from-background events, initial notification, and token refresh. |
| Use explicit app navigation intents; make pending intents immutable where supported and give destinations correct identity. | Route through the app coordinator; do not send arbitrary payload URLs to `UIApplication.open`. | Register background handlers at the SDK-required entry point; ordinary component effects are insufficient for that path. |
| Keep callback work bounded; delegate suitable longer work to an approved scheduler. | Call completion handlers on every path and respect background execution limits. | Retain unsubscribe functions and remove subscriptions with their owner. |

Android 13+ notification permission affects posting notifications; account for target/OS behavior in the actual app. FCM notification messages can be displayed by the OS while backgrounded. Apple's background notification delivery is not guaranteed. See [Android permissions](https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/compose/notifications/notification-permission), [FCM Android delivery](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/android/receive-messages), and [Apple background updates](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/pushing-background-updates-to-your-app).

### Typed payload boundary · React Native example

This sample uses a structured intent instead of opening a supplied URI. The payload is a navigation hint; the server remains authoritative about resource access.

